## Context
Author ruling, 2026-10-27: in `/lair`, any ticket that took more than 30 minutes to solve is pointed out, and a doctor visit is suggested for it. A slow ticket is a symptom of harness friction (review rounds, gate refusals, tooling faults). Raid 1229 had several: 1231 (three gaze rounds), 1232 and 1233 (gate refusals over seat evidence, ticket 1236), 1233 (five gaze rounds).

The doctors stay author-called: lair suggests, never starts, a visit (as `skills/test-doctor/SKILL.md` already allows "`/lair` and `/trace-doctor` may suggest one"). The suggestion hands the doctor its complaint: the ticket, its duration, and where the time went. Blocked by 1238, which makes `/harness-doctor` a complaint-driven visit.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] lair lists each ticket closed since the last lair whose solve time exceeded 30 minutes, with its duration and the source of that number (labelled measured, read from a record, or derived)
- [ ] "Solve time" is defined once, from data the harness already records (for example the merge request's opening to its MERGED read-back, or the hunt step clock), and the definition names its source; a ticket whose duration cannot be read is listed as unknown, never as fast
- [ ] For each listed ticket, lair suggests a visit with the complaint pre-filled: `/harness-doctor` when the time sat in review, gate or tooling; `/test-doctor` when it sat in test gates
- [ ] lair never starts a visit itself; the 30-minute threshold lives in one place
- [ ] A fast unit test covers the threshold with synthetic records whose timestamps are 29 and 31 minutes apart (no real waiting), and the unknown-duration case

## Raid annotation (Imagine+Plan, 2026-10-27)
- **Premise: holds.** `skills/lair/SKILL.md` has steps 0-15, none lists slow tickets; `skills/test-doctor/SKILL.md` l.21 already allows `/lair` to suggest; blocker 1238 closed. No script computes solve time (`scripts/step_clock.py` times one step in one session, persists nothing).
- **Solve time (recommend):** PR `createdAt` to `mergedAt` from the forge (read from a record, labelled so). Ticket log `created` to `closed` is wrong: 1232 sat 05:45 to 07:27 mostly blocked on 1230/1231, which is backlog wait, not solve. Link ticket to PR via the `Closed: autoclosed — PR #N` header (archived tickets in `tickets/closed/`). Forge access follows the `harness-extension-point` pattern of `skills/roar/check-close-claims.sh`. Tickets with no PR number, or no readable timestamps, print `unknown`. Step clock rejected as source: not persisted, so never available at lair time.
- **"Since the last lair":** no record of one exists; use the window of steps 14-15 (24 h). Say so in the output; do not invent a marker.
- **Simplest path:** one `scripts/slow_tickets.py`: constant `THRESHOLD_MIN = 30` (single home), pure `classify(records, threshold)` over `{ticket, pr, created, merged}` dicts, thin CLI that does the `gh pr view --json createdAt,mergedAt,statusCheckRollup` reads. One new lair step (16, after 15) quoting the output; suggestion only, no question, no launch (same negative wording as step 12/13). Antipatterns: no config knob, no new record file, no store of PR times; no "doctor router" abstraction.
- **Routing (decision for impl, cheap):** `/test-doctor` when the CI check span (derived: max completedAt less min startedAt of statusCheckRollup) is at least half the duration; else `/harness-doctor`. Complaint line = ticket, duration, source label, PR number, split. Lower-risk fallback if the author dislikes the heuristic: always `/harness-doctor`, with the check span shown.
- **Actions (ordered):** (1) write `tests/test_slow_tickets.py` first; (2) `scripts/slow_tickets.py` to pass it; (3) lair step 16 in `skills/lair/SKILL.md` + string-match ratchet in the style of `tests/test_lair_dream_suggestion.py` (no ask/wait/launch, threshold not restated in the skill text); (4) `make skills-catalog` only if frontmatter changes (it should not).
- **First failing test:** `classify` on synthetic records at 29 min (absent), 31 min (listed with duration + label "read from a record"), missing `merged` (listed `unknown`, never fast), plus routing at the half-CI boundary.
- **Tautological-test check:** do not assert the constant equals 30; assert behaviour at 29/31 so changing the constant must fail one. Assert the unknown case is in the output, not merely absent from "fast". The skill-text ratchet pins negatives only, as 0910 does. No test that calls `gh`.
- **Shared files:** `skills/lair/SKILL.md` (1227 gaze closing and 1246 sitrep may touch lair/session text; 1244 and 1240 do not, but 1244 owns `main_sweep.py`, which step 15 cites; do not edit it). Append step 16 at the end to keep conflicts to one hunk; rebase before gate. `tests/` additions are new files, no overlap. 1237 (adherence parsing) is disjoint.
- **Risk:** low; the one open call is the routing heuristic. Cost: about 1 coder-hour.

## Raid feasibility (2026-10-27)
WARN (data source). Paths hold: lair steps 0-15, `test_lair_dream_suggestion.py`, `check-close-claims.sh`, test-doctor l.21. 387 closed tickets carry `Closed: autoclosed — PR #N` and about 25 use other spellings ("Merged PR #N", "done — PR #N merged"), so the ticket-to-PR link must parse all of them or print unknown.
- Blind spot 1: since ticket 1224 (150473be, yesterday) gaze, verify-gate and merge post a measured `duration:` line as a PR comment. That is a persisted, measured split of review, gate and merge time, which criterion 3 routing needs. It is better than the derived CI-span heuristic, and the plan's claim that the step clock is "never available at lair time" no longer holds. Check: `gh pr view 1702 --json comments --jq '.comments[].body' | grep duration:`.
- Blind spot 2: PR `createdAt` to `mergedAt` also counts queue wait. Raid PRs sit open until serial merges finish, which is the same backlog bias the plan rejected for ticket logs. Label it as an upper bound, or use the sum of the duration lines.
- Overlap: lair step 13 already offers `/test-doctor` from `gate_traces.py`. Fold the test-doctor branch into it, or cite it, so lair does not make two test-doctor offers.
